 nowrap><b><font color="#FFFFFF" face="Trebuchet MS, Tahoma, Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="2">Lunkk</font></b></td>
                <td bgcolor="#333333" valign="top"><font color="#FFFFFF" face="Trebuchet MS, Tahoma, Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="2"><small>Posted&nbsp;-&nbsp;09/24/2004&nbsp;: 10:09:17 AM</small><hr size="1"><span class="spnMessageText"> <img src="http://www.monstarla.com/hobbies/gaming/moontemple.jpg" border="0"><br /><br />I posted this mainly so you can see the interface.  I wish I had more shots so you could see more of it.  A few things of note..<br /><br />1.  The hotkeys at the bottom go from 1 to +.  This gives you 12 keys instead of the standard 10 and you can easily scroll through hotbars.  The icon that says RUN is a macro I set up.  You pick the icon and text for each macro.<br /><br />2.  Just above the hotkeys you see a blue line which extends across the screen.  That is the experience bar and if you mouse-over it you see the exact number of experience you have and how much you need to level.  When you die you can go get your corpse or pay experience you be resurrected.  The amount you pay is listed as a number.<br /><br />3.  Above the experience bar you see a small icon that looks like two maces crossed.  That is the current "stance" my warrior is in.  As I leveled up further and completed a quest I got a second stance which then lead to more abilities.  When you press those buttons it immediately switches to a new hotkey bar where you can add the corresponding abilities to that stance.  The same type of interface is available for a rogue who is stealthed and also for paladin auras.  I'm sure its also available for druids who are in animal form, etc. <br /><br />4.  On the right of the hotkeys you see the major windows.  The dwarf in the picture is customized to your character's appearance and clicking on that icon brings up your character information and skills.  The book is your spells and special abilities.  You can easily drag from the spells window to your hotkey bar.  Third is the talent screens where you choose your talents after level 10.  Next is the quest button which journals all your active quests and what you've completed and what's left to do.  You can also view the full quest dialogue there.  I don't remember what the ! button does.  The globe opens your world map.  The computer is standard game settings and options and the question mark is the help menu and petitioning system.  Each of those can be opened with mouse or keyboard.<br /><br />5.  The bags on the right hold all your inventory.  You are restricted by the amount of items; weight doesn't matter.  As you adventure you find bags that hold more things.  There's also a bank in each major city where you can add more stuff and more bags.<br /><br />6.  At the top left you see your group members and they various buffs and effects that are on them.  By right clicking the portraits it brings up the standard group options to leave a group or kick a member or change loot rights, etc.  The crown indicates the group leader.  The top, larger portrait is your character.  To the right of that is the current target.  Notice the shield next to the portrait which indicates PvP status.  This is an alliance NPC.<br /><br />7.  To the far right at the top you see the name of the current area and your minimap.  On the minimap you see the group members indicated by green dots.  Quest NPCs show up on the mini-map as well.<br /><br /><font color="green">The Olive Grape</font id="green"><br />Molieren - Treasurer and Prophet<br /><img src="http://www.monstarla.com/images/moli.jpg" border="0"><br /></span></font></td>
